# Provenance for dtaa_dividend_rates.csv
#
# Vendored India DTAA dividend treatment data. Form 67 (CBDT Statement
# of income from a country or specified territory outside India and
# Foreign Tax Credit) uses it to populate:
#
#   * Column (9): Article No. of Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement
#   * Column (10): Rate of tax as per Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement
#
# Schedule FSI uses the separate relief article. The table also gates the
# §90 vs §91 vs "unknown" branching of the relief computation that
# populates Form 67 columns (11) / (12) / (13).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scope of v1
#
# v1 covers DIVIDEND-source income only (Form 67's
# Form67SourceOfIncome.DIVIDEND), matching the current Form 67
# compute layer's single-source v1 scope at
# src/ibkr_fa/compute/form67.py (the DIVIDEND-only emission is
# bounded by the Activity Statement's Dividends-section WHT
# coverage). Interest / royalty / fees-for-technical-services
# article + rate vendoring will be added as separate per-source CSVs
# or extended columns when Form 67 compute begins emitting non-
# DIVIDEND rows.
#
# v1 is also CONSERVATIVE on country coverage: only countries whose
# treaty article + rate (for DTAA entries) or whose ABSENCE from
# India's comprehensive DTAA list (for no_dtaa entries) we have
# directly verified against an authoritative source during this PR.
# This is the safe shape — see "Tri-state lookup semantics" below
# for how the loader distinguishes a vendored no_dtaa row from an
# unvendored (unknown) country.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# CSV schema (8 columns)
#
#   iso_alpha2                       ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code
#                                    (the lookup key; matches the
#                                    convention from
#                                    itr_country_codes.csv).
#   country_name                     Title-case display name.
#   applicability                    One of {dtaa, no_dtaa}. See
#                                    "Tri-state lookup semantics".
#   withholding_article              The DTAA article that caps the
#                                    source-country withholding on
#                                    dividends (e.g., "Article 10(2)(b)"
#                                    of the India-US DTAA). EMPTY for
#                                    no_dtaa entries.
#   relief_article                   The DTAA article that grants
#                                    India the right to allow the
#                                    foreign tax as a credit
#                                    (e.g., "Article 25(2)(a)" of the
#                                    India-US DTAA). EMPTY for
#                                    no_dtaa entries. Schedule FSI
#                                    renders this article; Form 67
#                                    col 9 renders withholding_article
#                                    beside the treaty-rate field.
#   portfolio_dividend_rate_percent  The treaty cap (percent) applied
#                                    to the source-state withholding
#                                    on the typical IBKR-investor
#                                    case: portfolio holdings of a
#                                    non-corporate beneficial owner
#                                    not exceeding the
#                                    "substantial holding" threshold
#                                    of the relevant article (almost
#                                    always 10% voting stock). For
#                                    dual-rate articles like
#                                    India-US Article 10 (15% for
#                                    >=10% corporate; 25% in all
#                                    other cases) we vendor the
#                                    NON-SUBSTANTIAL rate (25% for
#                                    US), per Article 10(2)(b)
#                                    verbatim — this is the rate
#                                    that applies to portfolio Indian
#                                    individuals via IBKR. EMPTY for
#                                    no_dtaa entries. Stored as a
#                                    decimal-friendly string so the
#                                    loader can parse via Decimal()
#                                    without float precision loss.
#   applicability_notes              Plain-text caveats: dual-rate
#                                    sub-clauses, REIT / RIC carve-
#                                    outs, TIEA-only relationships,
#                                    administrative-rate divergences,
#                                    etc. Surfaced into the loader's
#                                    typed entry for downstream
#                                    review-CSV rendering and
#                                    auditor reconciliation.
#   source_citation                  Per-row citation: official
#                                    treaty notification, IRS / CBDT
#                                    treaty-text URL, or — for
#                                    no_dtaa entries — the CBDT
#                                    DTAA-list URL with note that
#                                    the country is absent from
#                                    the comprehensive list.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tri-state lookup semantics (CRITICAL)
#
# The loader returns ONE of THREE states for a queried ISO
# alpha-2 code:
#
#   1. vendored, applicability = "dtaa"
#        → §90 / §90A pathway applies. The compute layer can populate
#          Form 67 col 9 (article) + col 10 (rate). v1 writer wiring
#          uses withholding_article for col 9; Schedule FSI uses
#          relief_article for its relief-article field.
#
#   2. vendored, applicability = "no_dtaa"
#        → §91 unilateral relief pathway applies (or no relief is
#          available, depending on whether the source country
#          actually taxes the dividend). Form 67 col 9 + col 10 are
#          left EMPTY per the model invariant
#          (section_91_credit_inr requires both DTAA fields to be
#          None at the model layer).
#
#   3. NOT vendored (lookup returns None)
#        → UNKNOWN. The compute layer must NOT silently default to
#          §91; doing so would mis-attribute relief for any DTAA
#          country we have not yet vendored. v1 contract: leave
#          Form 67 cols 9-13 None for unknown countries and surface
#          the unvendored-country list via a downstream advisory
#          (the post-#221 review pattern). This is the BLOCKING
#          design point flagged by the step-4/N planning rubber-
#          duck — absence-equals-no-DTAA is dangerous given partial
#          coverage.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sources verified during initial PR
#
# US (Article 10(2)(b) 25%; Article 25(2)(a) relief): verbatim
# verified against the IRS-hosted Convention Between the United
# States of America and India PDF
# https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-trty/india.pdf (signed 1989-09-12,
# entered into force 1990-12-18). The PDF was fetched on
# 2026-06-05 (2.5MB, PDF 1.6) and pdftotext -layout extracted
# cleanly. Article 10 verbatim text at lines 1177-1292 of the
# extracted layout (Article 11 begins at line 1293); Article 25
# at lines 2351-2464 (Article 26 begins at line 2465). The 25%
# rate is the cap for "all other cases" (Article 10(2)(b)),
# including portfolio Indian individuals; 15% (Article 10(2)(a))
# applies ONLY to corporate beneficial owners holding >= 10%
# voting stock. The PDF further specifies that 10(2)(b) (not
# 10(2)(a)) applies to dividends from US Regulated Investment
# Companies, and that 10(2)(b) applies to dividends from US Real
# Estate Investment Trusts where the individual beneficial owner
# holds < 10%.
#
# KY / BM (no_dtaa): checked on 2026-11-11 against CBDT's DTAA list
# at
# https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/en/web/guest/double-taxation-avoidance-agreements
# The page renders its list in the browser from the site's search
# API, so the list was fetched (wget with a browser user agent) as
# the page's own request: POST
# https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/o/search/v1.0/search?nestedFields=embedded&page=1&pageSize=-1
# with the blueprint DTAA_FULL_TREATY_BP_ERC and dtaa_active true
# (173 active records, 97 of them Comprehensive Agreements). The
# same query with dtaa_active false returned the 43 superseded
# "(Old)" records. Neither the Cayman Islands nor Bermuda has a
# comprehensive or limited agreement in either list. Each is listed
# only under Tax Information Exchange Agreement (TIEA), which
# provides exchange of information, not relief on doubly taxed
# income:
#   * Bermuda: notified under section 90 by Notification No. 5/2011
#     [F. No. 503/2/2009-FTD-I] dated 2011-01-24,
#     https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/w/bermuda-tax-information-exchange-agreement-tiea-1
#   * Cayman Islands: notified under section 90 by Notification
#     No. 61/2011 [F. No. 503/03/2009-FTD-I], S.O. 2902(E), dated
#     2011-12-27,
#     https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/w/cayman-islands-tax-information-exchange-agreement-tiea-1
#
# HK (Article 10(2) 5%; Article 23(2)(a) relief): until #1146 this
# row was no_dtaa, checked only against secondary sources. India and
# the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the People's
# Republic of China signed a comprehensive DTAA on 19 March 2018. It
# entered into force on 30 November 2018, and India notified it
# under §90(1) by CBDT Notification No. 89/2018, S.O. 6247(E)
# [F. No. 500/124/97-FTD-II], dated 2018-12-21. Under Article
# 29(3)(b) it has effect in India for income derived in any fiscal
# year beginning on or after 1 April 2019, that is FY 2019-20
# onward. Sources, fetched on 2026-11-11:
#   * CBDT's DTAA list (as for KY / BM above) has "Hong Kong :
#     Comprehensive Agreements", entry into force 2018-11-30. Its
#     record, https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/w/hong-kong-comprehensive-agreements-1,
#     carries the notification ("NOTIFICATION: NO. S.O. 6247(E)
#     [NO.89/2018/F.NO.500/124/97-FTD-II], DATED 21-12-2018") and
#     the Agreement text, Articles 10 and 23 included.
#   * The Inland Revenue Department's synthesised text of the
#     Agreement as modified by the MLI,
#     https://www.ird.gov.hk/eng/pdf/Synthesised_Text_HKSAR_India.pdf
#     (393,743 bytes; PDF 1.6; 39 pages); pdftotext -layout
#     extracted cleanly. Article 10 verbatim text at lines 681-735
#     (Article 11 begins at line 736); Article 23 at lines
#     1206-1253 (Article 24 begins at line 1254). IRD's treaty table
#     (https://www.ird.gov.hk/eng/tax/dta_inc.htm) gives the same
#     dates: signed 19.03.2018, in force 30.11.2018.
#
# Article 10(2) reads verbatim "... if the beneficial owner of the
# dividends is a resident of the other Contracting Party, the tax so
# charged shall not exceed 5 per cent of the gross amount of the
# dividends." That is one rate for every beneficial owner type, so
# col 9 cites "Article 10(2)" with no sub-paragraph. Within Article
# 10 the MLI changes only paragraph 6, the main-purpose rule
# ("SUPERSEDED by paragraph 1 of Article 7 of the MLI to the extent
# of incompatibility"); the 5% rate is unchanged. Article 23(2)(a)
# is India's credit clause: "India shall allow as a deduction from
# the tax on the income of that resident, an amount equal to the tax
# paid in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region", capped at
# the Indian tax attributable to that income.
#
# In practice Hong Kong doesn't withhold tax on dividends, because
# dividends are not chargeable to tax in Hong Kong (IRD
# Departmental Interpretation and Practice Notes No. 44 (Revised),
# paragraph 86, https://www.ird.gov.hk/eng/pdf/dipn44.pdf). Any Hong
# Kong tax that is withheld goes down the §90 route. The old row's
# note that Hong Kong income might be relieved through the
# India-China DTAA is dropped: the India-Hong Kong Agreement is the
# treaty that applies.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sources verified in country-expansion sub-PRs
#
# SG (Article 10(2)(b) 15%; Article 25(2) relief): verbatim verified
# against the taxsutra-hosted Singapore-India DTAA PDF
# https://www.taxsutra.com/sites/taxsutra.com/files/dtaa/SINGAPORE.pdf
# (notified GSR 610(E) dated 1994-08-08). The PDF was fetched on
# 2026-06-06 (159KB; PDF 1.5; 18 pages) and pdftotext -layout
# extracted cleanly. Article 10 verbatim text at lines 249-290 of
# the extracted layout (Article 11 begins at line 291); Article 25
# at lines 567-617 (Article 26 begins at line 618). The 15% rate
# is the cap for "all other cases" (Article 10(2)(b)); 10% (Article
# 10(2)(a)) applies ONLY to corporate beneficial owners owning >=
# 25% of the shares of the Singapore distributor. The 2005 and
# 2016 Protocols (the latter rewriting Article 13 on capital gains)
# and the MLI (BEPS principal-purpose test) did NOT amend the
# Article 10 dividend rates. In practice Singapore has not imposed
# a withholding tax on dividends paid to non-residents since 2003
# (one-tier corporate tax system); the typical IBKR-individual case
# has 0% Singapore withholding so the FTC computation yields zero
# foreign tax to credit; the entry is vendored for completeness and
# to handle the rare case where Singapore-source dividend is
# nonetheless withheld.
#
# IE (Article 10(2) 10%; Article 23(2) relief): verbatim verified
# against the aseanbriefing-hosted India-Ireland DTAA PDF
# https://www.aseanbriefing.com/userfiles/resources-pdfs/India/DTA/Asia_DTA_Ireland_India.pdf
# (notified Notification No. 45/2002 [F. No. 503/6/99-FTD] dated
# 2002-02-20; Irish counterpart S.I. No. 521/2001). The PDF was
# fetched on 2026-06-06 (786KB; PDF 1.3; 8 pages) and pdftotext
# -layout extracted cleanly. Article 10 verbatim text at lines
# 324-353 of the extracted layout (Article 11 begins at line 354);
# Article 23 at lines 629-682 (Article 24 begins at line 683).
# The 10% rate is a SINGLE-RATE cap (no substantial-holder split
# unlike India-US 15%/25% or India-Singapore 10%/15%) so the
# vendored value is the literal Article 10(2) rate applying to
# every beneficial owner type. Ireland is the dominant UCITS-ETF
# domicile (VWRA / IUSA / EIMI / IWDA etc.); under Ireland's
# Investment Undertaking Tax regime + non-resident exemption
# distributions from Irish UCITS funds to non-Irish-resident
# investors are typically not subject to Irish dividend WHT so the
# typical IBKR-individual FTC computation yields zero foreign tax
# to credit; the entry remains vendored for completeness and the
# rare case where Irish-source dividend is nonetheless withheld.
#
# CA (Article 10(2)(b) 25%; Article 23(3)(a) relief): verbatim
# verified against the taxsutra-hosted India-Canada DTAA PDF
# https://www.taxsutra.com/sites/taxsutra.com/files/dtaa/CANADA%20DTAA.pdf
# (notified S.O. 28(E) dated 1998-01-15 effective from 1997-05-06;
# the PDF's line 16 reads verbatim "Notification : No. SO 28(E),
# dated 15-1-1998"). The PDF was fetched on 2026-06-06 (155KB;
# PDF 1.4; 23 pages) and pdftotext -layout extracted cleanly.
# Article 10 verbatim text at lines 312-344 of the extracted layout
# (Article 11 begins at line 345); Article 23 at lines 593-657
# (Article 24 begins at line 659). The 25% rate is the cap for
# "all other cases" (Article 10(2)(b)); 15% (Article 10(2)(a))
# applies ONLY to corporate beneficial owners controlling directly
# or indirectly at least 10% of the voting power in the Canadian
# distributor — structurally identical to the India-US DTAA
# Article 10 split. Note India-Canada's Article 23 is split into
# separate paragraphs for Canadian-resident relief (paragraph 2)
# and Indian-resident relief (paragraph 3) — the latter is the
# operative paragraph for Indian residents claiming credit on
# Canadian-source income; its sub-paragraph (a) is the dividend /
# income credit clause (vs (b) which is the capital credit clause).
# This differs from India-US Article 25 which puts the Indian-
# resident clause in 25(2) and India-Singapore Article 25 which
# has a single shared Indian-resident clause.
#
# DE (Article 10(2) 10%; Article 23(2) relief): verbatim verified
# against the dsa-library-hosted India-Germany DTAA PDF
# https://s3-ap-northeast-1.amazonaws.com/dsa-library/Resources/3685/Asia_DTA_Germany_India.pdf
# (notified S.O. 836(E) dated 1996-11-29; the PDF's line 29 reads
# verbatim "Notification No. S. O. 836(E), dtd. 29.11.1996"). The
# PDF was fetched on 2026-06-06 (811KB; PDF 1.3; 8 pages) and
# pdftotext -layout extracted cleanly. Article 10 verbatim text at
# lines 317-351 of the extracted layout (Article 11 begins at line
# 352); Article 23 at lines 622-689 (Article 24 begins at line
# 690). The 10% rate is a SINGLE-RATE cap (no substantial-holder
# split — structurally similar to India-Ireland's Article 10(2)
# flat 10% cap; different from India-US 15%/25% or India-Singapore
# 10%/15% two-tier structures). The treaty has NO most-favoured-
# nation (MFN) clause (verified absent from the PDF body via
# searches for "most-favoured" / "most-favored" / "MFN" / "third
# State" — none match), so Germany is unaffected by the 2023
# Indian Supreme Court Nestlé-case MFN ruling that addressed
# Netherlands / France / Switzerland. Germany's Article 23(2) is
# the Indian-resident clause; it is a single paragraph (no sub-
# paragraphs unlike India-US Article 25(2)(a) or India-Canada
# Article 23(3)(a)). Germany levies non-resident WHT on dividends
# at 25% domestic law + 5.5% solidarity surcharge; the DTAA cap
# of 10% reduces this for Indian portfolio individuals via the
# German tax-relief application process.
#
# JP (Article 10(2) 15%; Article 23(2)(a) relief): verbatim
# verified against the aseanbriefing-hosted India-Japan DTAA PDF
# https://www.aseanbriefing.com/userfiles/resources-pdfs/India/DTA/Asia_DTA_Japan_India.pdf
# (notified G.S.R. 101(E) dated 1990-03-01; the PDF's line 29
# reads verbatim "Notification No. G. S. R. 101(E), dtd. 1st
# March, 1990"). The PDF was fetched on 2026-06-06 (754KB; PDF
# 1.3; 8 pages) and pdftotext -layout extracted cleanly. Article
# 10 verbatim text at lines 283-314 (Article 11 begins at line
# 315); Article 23 at lines 563-602 (Article 24 begins at line
# 603). The 15% rate is a SINGLE-RATE cap (Article 10(2) — no
# substantial-holder split) — higher than the more modern
# India-Germany / India-Ireland 10% single-rate treaties because
# this is one of India's older treaties (1989 / notified 1990)
# before the international trend toward lower dividend rates.
# The treaty has NO MFN clause (verified absent from the PDF
# body; also Japan is NOT on the CBDT Circular 3/2022 list of 6
# MFN-clause treaties: Netherlands / France / Switzerland /
# Sweden / Spain / Hungary). Article 23 in India-Japan is split
# into separate paragraphs for India-resident relief (paragraph 2
# sub-paragraphs (a)/(b)) and Japan-resident relief (paragraph 3
# sub-paragraphs (a)/(b)/(c)); the Indian-resident credit clause
# is 23(2)(a). Japan levies non-resident WHT on dividends at
# 15.315% (15% domestic + 2.1% special reconstruction surcharge)
# under domestic law; the DTAA cap of 15% slightly lowers this
# for Indian portfolio individuals.
#
# IMPORTANT METHODOLOGY NOTE: web-search summaries for India-Japan
# DTAA returned THREE incorrect values during initial research:
#   * claimed rate 10% (actual 15%);
#   * claimed notification S.O. 789(E) (actual G.S.R. 101(E));
#   * claimed date 1990-08-10 (actual 1990-03-01).
# All three were corrected via direct PDF verification.
# Reinforces the established convention: ALWAYS verify against
# the primary PDF — never trust web-search treaty summaries for
# rates / article numbers / notification IDs.
#
# NL (Article 10(2) 10%; Article 23(4) relief): verbatim verified
# against the taxsutra-hosted India-Netherlands DTAA PDF
# https://www.taxsutra.com/sites/taxsutra.com/files/dtaa/NETHERLANDS.pdf
# (notified G.S.R. 382(E) dated 1989-03-27 amended by S.O. 693(E)
# dated 1999-08-30 and Notification 2/2013 dated 2013-01-14; the
# PDF's lines 14-15 read verbatim "Notification : No. GSR 382(E),
# dated 27-3-1989 as amended by Notification No. SO 693(E) dated
# 30-8-1999 and Notification No. 2/2013 dated 14-1-2013"). The
# PDF was fetched on 2026-06-06 (162KB; PDF 1.5; 20 pages) and
# pdftotext -layout extracted cleanly. Article 10 verbatim text
# at lines 300-330 (Article 11 begins at line 331); Article 23
# at lines 607-661 (Article 24 begins at line 663). The 10% rate
# is the post-1999-protocol single-rate cap — the original 1989
# treaty had a 10%/15% sub-paragraph split which the 1999
# Protocol (S.O. 693(E)) REPLACED with a single 10% rate; the
# PDF marks the substitution with footnote brackets around the
# current Article 10(2) text.
#
# NL is the FIRST MFN-list country vendored. Per CBDT Circular
# 3/2022 India has MFN clauses with 6 treaty partners only:
# Netherlands / France / Switzerland / Sweden / Spain / Hungary.
# The Protocol Section IV paragraph 2 (lines 850-856 of the extract) carries
# the famous MFN clause that triggered the 2023 Indian Supreme
# Court Nestlé-case ruling. Indian taxpayers historically argued
# the MFN clause triggers a 5% rate via post-OECD-membership
# treaties with Slovenia / Lithuania / Colombia; the Supreme
# Court resolved this in favour of the literal 10% rate:
#   (i) MFN auto-application requires the third State to have
#       been an OECD member at the time of its India treaty
#       signature (not retrospectively after joining OECD later);
#   (ii) Even when (i) is met, MFN application requires explicit
#       CBDT notification (India has not issued such a
#       notification for Netherlands).
# Vendoring the literal 10% rate per the Supreme Court position.
# The applicability_notes document the MFN context so a future
# auditor reading the row can understand the Nestlé context
# without re-tracing the litigation history.
#
# Article 23 in India-Netherlands has paragraphs 1-3 covering
# Netherlands-resident relief and paragraph 4 covering Indian-
# resident relief — so the Schedule FSI relief citation
# is "Article 23(4)" (not 23(2)(a) like India-Japan or 23(3)(a)
# like India-Canada).
#
# IL (Article 10(2) 10%; Article 24(2)(a) relief; tax-sparing
# 24(3)): verbatim verified against the aseanbriefing-hosted
# India-Israel DTAA PDF
# https://www.aseanbriefing.com/userfiles/resources-pdfs/India/DTA/Asia_DTA_Israel_India.pdf
# (notified G.S.R. 256(E) dated 1996-06-26 effective from
# 1996-05-15; the PDF's line 29 reads verbatim "Notification No.
# G. S. R. 256(E), dtd.26.06.1996"). The PDF was fetched on
# 2026-06-06 (800KB; PDF 1.3; 8 pages) and pdftotext -layout
# extracted cleanly. Article 10 verbatim text at lines 325-356
# (Article 11 begins at line 357); Article 24 (Elimination of
# Double Taxation) at lines 674-708 (Article 25 begins at line
# 709). The 10% rate is a SINGLE-RATE cap (Article 10(2) — no
# substantial-holder split) — similar in structure to
# India-Germany / India-Ireland flat 10% caps and different from
# the India-US (15%/25%) or India-Singapore (10%/15%) two-tier
# structures.
#
# The treaty has NO MFN clause (verified absent from the PDF
# body via search for "most-favoured" / "MFN" / "third State" /
# "OECD" — none match; also Israel is NOT on the CBDT Circular
# 3/2022 list of 6 MFN-clause treaties: Netherlands / France /
# Switzerland / Sweden / Spain / Hungary) so Israel is
# unaffected by the 2023 Indian Supreme Court Nestlé-case
# ruling.
#
# Article 24 in India-Israel is structured with paragraph 1
# covering Israel-resident relief and paragraph 2 covering
# Indian-resident relief; paragraph 2(a) is the Indian-resident
# credit clause for income tax and paragraph 2(b) for capital
# tax (out of Form 67 scope). The Schedule FSI relief citation is
# therefore "Article 24(2)(a)" — not 23(2)(a) like
# India-Japan (which numbers its elimination article as 23 not
# 24) or 23(3)(a) like India-Canada.
#
# NOTABLE TAX-SPARING PROVISION (Article 24(3)): the India-
# Israel treaty contains an override clause "Notwithstanding the
# provisions of paragraphs 1 and 2" that grants a deemed 15%
# credit on dividends regardless of the actual foreign tax paid.
# Verbatim: "where a resident of a Contracting State derives
# income by way of dividends on shares of companies resident in
# the other Contracting State, the first-mentioned Contracting
# State shall allow credit of 15 per cent. of the gross amount
# of such dividend from the tax payable" (PDF lines 695-699).
# This is symmetric tax-sparing: an Indian resident receiving
# Israeli dividends may claim 15% deemed credit even when Israel
# only withholds the 10% Article 10(2) cap.
#
# Vendoring decision: the relief_article column carries the
# standard Article 24(2)(a) (not 24(3)) because the tool never
# claims a credit above the foreign tax actually withheld: the §90
# credit at src/ibkr_fa/compute/form67.py is min(column 7,
# eligible foreign tax), and eligible foreign tax is at most the
# Rule-128-converted withholding. The tool has no input for a
# deemed (tax-sparing) credit: a [[form67.tax_payable_overrides]]
# entry supplies column 7 only and cannot raise the credit above
# eligible foreign tax. Claiming the 24(3) tax-sparing benefit
# above the actual foreign WHT is manual work outside the tool;
# the row's applicability_notes document the 24(3) override so a
# future auditor reading the vendored row can apply for the tax-
# sparing benefit independently.
#
# Israel domestic non-resident WHT on dividends is typically 25%
# under the Israeli Income Tax Ordinance; the DTAA cap of 10%
# reduces this for Indian portfolio individuals via the Israeli
# tax-relief application process at source upon presentation of
# an Indian tax-residency certificate (Form 10F).
#
# AE (Article 10(2) 10%; Article 25(2) relief): verbatim verified
# against the taxsutra-hosted India-UAE DTAA PDF
# https://www.taxsutra.com/sites/taxsutra.com/files/dtaa/UAE%20DTAA.pdf
# (notified G.S.R. 710(E) dated 1993-11-18 as amended by S.O.
# 2001(E) dated 2007-11-28; the PDF's line 16 reads verbatim
# "Notification : No. GSR 710(E), dated 18-11-1993, as amended by
# Notification No. SO 2001(E), dated 28-11-2007"). The PDF was
# fetched on 2026-06-06 (113KB; PDF 1.4; 13 pages) and pdftotext
# -layout extracted cleanly. Article 10 verbatim text at lines
# 232-253 (Article 11 begins at line 254); Article 25 at lines
# 503-531 (Article 26 begins at line 532). The 10% rate is a
# SINGLE-RATE cap (Article 10(2) — no substantial-holder split)
# — similar in structure to India-Germany / India-Ireland /
# India-Israel flat 10% caps.
#
# The literal Article 10(2) text is uncharacteristically terse —
# verbatim it reads "the tax so charged shall not exceed 10 per
# cent." (with period; the "of the gross amount of the
# dividends" base is implicit). Compare Article 12(2) of the same
# treaty which does spell out "10 per cent of the gross amount of
# such royalties". This brevity is unique to the India-UAE
# treaty and does NOT indicate ambiguity — practitioners and the
# CBDT both treat the rate as 10% of gross dividends.
#
# The treaty has NO MFN clause (verified absent from the PDF
# body via search for "most-favoured" / "MFN" / "third State" /
# "OECD" — none match; also UAE is NOT on the CBDT Circular
# 3/2022 list of 6 MFN-clause treaties: Netherlands / France /
# Switzerland / Sweden / Spain / Hungary) so UAE is unaffected
# by the 2023 Indian Supreme Court Nestlé-case ruling.
#
# Article 25 is structured with paragraph 1 = general savings
# clause; paragraph 2 = Indian-resident credit clause (income
# tax + capital tax + special surtax sub-clause for corporate
# residents); paragraph 3 = UAE-resident credit clause;
# paragraph 4 = UNILATERAL tax-sparing provision explicitly
# scoped "For the purpose of paragraph (3)" — deeming Indian
# income-tax-incentive exemptions as if paid for paragraph 3
# purposes (applies ONLY to UAE-residents receiving Indian
# income; NOT to Indian residents receiving UAE income;
# therefore NOT relevant to this Indian-resident dataset). The
# Schedule FSI relief citation is therefore "Article 25(2)" —
# paragraph 2 covers all dividend
# credit cases without sub-paragraph split (different from
# India-Israel Article 24(2)(a) which DOES split income tax /
# capital tax across sub-paragraphs).
#
# UAE is a 0%-individual-income-tax jurisdiction; UAE corporate
# tax introduced in 2023 (9% on taxable corporate profits above
# AED 375k) does not withhold on dividends paid to non-residents.
# Practical FTC for Indian-resident IBKR investors in UAE-listed
# shares (DFM / ADX) typically yields zero foreign tax to credit;
# the entry is vendored for tri-state lookup completeness and
# to handle the rare case where UAE-source income is processed
# through Form 67.
#
# The 2007 amendment notification S.O. 2001(E) introduced
# tie-breaker residency rules in Article 4 plus a revised
# Article 28 (Exchange of Information) and added Article 29
# (Limitation of Benefits); it did NOT amend Article 10 rates.
# The current consolidated treaty text (as extracted from the
# taxsutra PDF) carries the post-amendment article numbering
# and content. Per the established convention (S.O. vs G.S.R.
# notification series distinction) the original notification is
# G.S.R. 710(E) and the 2007 amendment is S.O. 2001(E) — both
# series are correctly cited verbatim in the source_citation.
#
# FR (Article 11(2) 10%; Article 25(1)(a) relief; MFN-list
# country #2): verbatim verified against the taxsutra-hosted
# India-France DTAA PDF
# https://www.taxsutra.com/sites/taxsutra.com/files/dtaa/FRANCE%20DTAA.pdf
# (notified Notification No. 9602 [F. No. 501/16/80-FTD] dated
# 1994-09-06 as amended by S.O. 650(E) dated 2000-07-10; the
# PDF's line 13 reads verbatim "Notification No. 9602 [F. No.
# 501/16/80-FTD], dated 6-9-1994, as amended by Notification No.
# SO 650(E), dated 10-7-2000"). The PDF was fetched on 2026-06-06
# (138KB; PDF 1.4; 16 pages) and pdftotext -layout extracted
# cleanly. Article 11 verbatim text at lines 240-281 (Article 12
# begins at line 282); Article 25 at lines 517-583 (Article 26
# begins at line 584); Protocol Section 7 (MFN clause) at lines
# 736-744.
#
# NOTABLE ARTICLE NUMBERING: in this treaty Dividends is
# Article 11 (NOT Article 10 as in most Indian DTAAs); Article
# 10 is Associated Enterprises. The withholding_article field
# is therefore "Article 11(2)" — different from US/IE/SG/CA/
# DE/JP/NL/IL/AE which all use Article 10(2) or 10(2)(b). The
# relief_article is "Article 25(1)(a)" — Article 25 has
# paragraph 1 = Indian-resident relief sub-paragraphs (a)(b);
# paragraph 2 = French-resident relief sub-paragraphs (a)-(f).
# Paragraph 1(a) is the Indian-resident credit clause for
# income (paragraph 1 also covers capital tax out of scope).
#
# FR is the second MFN-list country vendored (after NL). Per
# CBDT Circular 3/2022 India has MFN clauses with 6 treaty
# partners: Netherlands / France / Switzerland / Sweden / Spain
# / Hungary. The Protocol Section 7 of this treaty carries the
# MFN clause verbatim: "In respect of articles 11 (Dividends);
# 12 (Interest) and 13 ... if under any Convention; Agreement
# or Protocol signed after 1-9-1989; between India and a third
# State which is a member of the OECD; India limits its taxation
# at source on dividends ... to a rate lower or a scope more
# restricted than the rate or scope provided for in this
# Convention ... the same rate or scope ... shall also apply
# under this Convention".
#
# Indian taxpayers historically argued the MFN clause triggers
# a 5% rate via later India-Slovenia / India-Lithuania /
# India-Colombia treaties (after those third States joined the
# OECD). The 2023 Indian Supreme Court Nestlé-case ruling
# (Civil Appeal 1420/2023) resolved this in favour of the
# literal 10% rate per Article 11(2):
#   (i) MFN auto-application requires the third State to have
#       been an OECD member at the time of its India treaty
#       signature (not retrospectively after joining OECD later);
#   (ii) Even when (i) is met, MFN application requires explicit
#       CBDT notification (India has not issued such a
#       notification for France).
# Vendoring the literal 10% rate per the Supreme Court position.
# The applicability_notes document the MFN context so a future
# auditor reading the row can understand the Nestlé context
# without re-tracing the litigation history.
#
# Article 11(3)-(4) contain historic French "avoir fiscal"
# tax-credit-imputation provisions and prepayment (precompte)
# refund provisions — these are now largely defunct since the
# French 2004 tax reform abolished the avoir fiscal system; for
# vendoring purposes only the Article 11(2) rate matters.
#
# France is the second-largest European holding-company /
# equities jurisdiction (LVMH / TotalEnergies / Sanofi /
# L'Oréal / BNP Paribas / Air Liquide / EssilorLuxottica /
# Schneider Electric etc.); high IBKR exposure for Indian
# investors seeking European equity diversification.
#
# SE (Article 10(2) 10%; Article 24(2)(a) relief; MFN-list
# country #3): verbatim verified against the taxsutra-hosted
# India-Sweden DTAA PDF
# https://www.taxsutra.com/sites/taxsutra.com/files/dtaa/SWEDEN%20DTAA.pdf
# (notified G.S.R. 705(E) dated 1997-12-17; the PDF's line 14
# reads verbatim "Notification : No. GSR 705(E), dated
# 17-12-1997"). The PDF was fetched on 2026-06-06 (126KB; PDF
# 1.4; 14 pages) and pdftotext -layout extracted cleanly.
# Article 10 verbatim text at lines 246-269 (Article 11 begins
# at line 271); Article 24 at lines 501-549 (Article 25 begins
# at line 550); MFN clause in Protocol "With reference to
# Articles 10, 11 and 12" at lines 702-708.
#
# Standard treaty structure: Article 10 = Dividends with a flat
# 10% Article 10(2) cap (no substantial-holder split); Article
# 24 = Elimination of Double Taxation with paragraph 1 = general
# savings clause; paragraph 2 = Indian-resident relief
# sub-paragraphs (a)(b)(c) where (a) is the income-tax credit
# clause (b) covers exempt-income rate-progression and (c)
# covers capital tax (out of Form 67 scope); paragraph 3 =
# Swedish-resident relief sub-paragraphs (a)-(f) including
# tax-sparing provisions in (d)-(e) (NOT relevant to Form 67
# vendoring — applies only to Swedish residents receiving
# Indian income).
#
# Sweden IS the THIRD MFN-list country vendored (after NL #241
# and FR #244). Per CBDT Circular 3/2022 India has MFN clauses
# with 6 treaty partners: Netherlands / France / Switzerland /
# Sweden / Spain / Hungary. The Protocol carries the MFN clause
# verbatim:
#
#   "In respect of Articles 10 (Dividends), 11 (Interest) and 12
#   (Royalties and fees for technical services) if under any
#   Convention. Agreement or Protocol between India and a third
#   State which is a member of the OECD, India limits its
#   taxation at source on dividends, interest, royalties, or
#   fees for technical services to a rate lower or a scope more
#   restricted than the rate or scope provided for in this
#   Convention on the said items of income, the same rate or
#   scope as provided for in that Convention, Agreement or
#   Protocol on the said items of income shall also apply under
#   this Convention".
#
# UNIQUE FEATURE: unlike India-France (Protocol Section 7 with
# explicit "1-9-1989" cutoff date) and India-Netherlands
# (Protocol Section IV.2 with "after the signature of this
# convention" cutoff phrase), the India-Sweden MFN clause has
# NO explicit date cutoff. It applies to "any Convention,
# Agreement or Protocol between India and a third State which
# is a member of the OECD" without temporal bound.
#
# The same 2023 Indian Supreme Court Nestlé-case ruling
# framework applies (Civil Appeal 1420/2023):
#   (i) MFN auto-application requires the third State to have
#       been an OECD member at the time of its India treaty
#       signature (not retrospectively after joining OECD later);
#   (ii) Even when (i) is met, MFN application requires explicit
#       CBDT notification (India has not issued such a
#       notification for Sweden).
# Vendoring the literal 10% rate per the Supreme Court position.
#
# Sweden has Volvo / Ericsson / Atlas Copco / H&M / SEB /
# Investor AB / Sandvik / Hexagon as the major IBKR-accessible
# equities; significant Nordic-equity exposure for Indian
# investors.
#
# ES (Article 11(2) 15%; Article 25(2)(a) relief; MFN-list
# country #4 — but MFN clause covers ONLY royalties/FTS not
# dividends): verbatim verified against the taxsutra-hosted
# India-Spain DTAA PDF
# https://www.taxsutra.com/sites/taxsutra.com/files/dtaa/SPAIN%20DTAA.pdf
# (notified G.S.R. 356(E) dated 1995-04-21; the PDF's line 12
# reads verbatim "Notification : No. GSR 356(E), dated
# 21-4-1995"). The PDF was fetched on 2026-06-06 (119KB; PDF
# 1.4; 14 pages) and pdftotext -layout extracted cleanly.
# Article 11 verbatim text at lines 230-253 (Article 12 begins
# at line 254); Article 25 at lines 464-517 (Article 26 begins
# at line 518); MFN clause (royalties/FTS only — NOT dividends)
# in Protocol Section 7 at lines 643-651.
#
# UNUSUAL ARTICLE NUMBERING (like India-France): in this treaty
# Dividends is Article 11 (NOT Article 10 as in most Indian
# DTAAs); Article 10 is Associated Enterprises. The
# withholding_article field is therefore "Article 11(2)" — same
# pattern as India-France. Among 12 vendored countries the
# Article-11-for-dividends pattern applies to FR (10%) and ES
# (15%).
#
# RATE 15% — Spain has a HIGHER dividend rate than the modern
# 10% caps. The treaty was signed 1993-02-08 and notified
# 1995-04-21; it predates the international trend toward lower
# dividend WHT rates. Other 15%-rate vendored countries are
# India-Japan (also 1989-era treaty) and India-Singapore (the
# (2)(b) sub-paragraph rate for portfolio holders). The 10%
# rate applies to India-Germany / India-Ireland / India-Israel
# / India-Netherlands / India-Sweden / India-UAE / India-France
# (post-amendments where applicable).
#
# Article 25 has paragraph 1 = general savings clause;
# paragraph 2 = Indian-resident relief sub-paragraph (a) which
# is the income-tax credit clause with (i) and (ii)
# sub-sub-paragraphs splitting income tax / capital tax
# (capital tax (ii) is out of Form 67 scope) and (b) covers
# exempt-income rate-progression; paragraph 3 = Spanish-
# resident relief sub-paragraphs (a)(b)(c); paragraphs 4-5 =
# tax-sparing for Spanish residents (NOT relevant — applies
# only to Spanish residents receiving Indian income). The Schedule
# FSI relief citation is "Article 25(2)(a)" — structurally similar to
# India-US / India-Sweden / India-Japan / India-Israel which
# all use Article 24/25 paragraph 2(a).
#
# Spain IS the FOURTH MFN-list country vendored (after NL #241
# / FR #244 / SE #245). Per CBDT Circular 3/2022 India has MFN
# clauses with 6 treaty partners: Netherlands / France /
# Switzerland / Sweden / Spain / Hungary.
#
# CRITICAL NUANCE — the India-Spain MFN clause (Protocol
# Section 7) covers ONLY royalties and fees for technical
# services; it does NOT extend to dividends. Verbatim:
#
#   "However, if under any Convention or Agreement between
#   India and a third State which is a Member of the OECD,
#   which enters into force after 1-1-1990, India limits its
#   taxation at source on royalties or fees for technical
#   services to a rate lower or a scope more restricted than
#   the rate or scope provided for in this Convention on the
#   said items of incomes, the same rate or scope as provided
#   for in that Convention or Agreement on the said items of
#   income shall also apply under this Convention with effect
#   from the date on which the present Convention comes into
#   force or the relevant Indian Convention or Agreement,
#   whichever enters into force later."
#
# The MFN clause specifically enumerates only "royalties or
# fees for technical services" — dividends are NOT included.
# Therefore the 2023 Indian Supreme Court Nestlé-case ruling
# (which addressed dividend MFN auto-application via NL/FR
# treaties) does NOT affect the India-Spain dividend rate
# vendoring. The literal 15% dividend rate per Article 11(2)
# stands without MFN considerations.
#
# This is the FIRST vendored MFN-list country where the MFN
# clause does NOT cover dividends. Spain is on the
# CBDT-Circular-3/2022 list because it HAS an MFN clause (for
# royalties); but the MFN-Nestlé framework that protected the
# literal NL/FR/SE 10% dividend rates is INAPPLICABLE here
# because Spain's MFN clause never reached dividends in the
# first place. The vendored 15% rate is simply the literal
# Article 11(2) rate; no Nestlé citation is needed.
#
# Spain has Inditex (Zara) / Iberdrola / Banco Santander /
# Telefónica / BBVA / Repsol / Amadeus / Aena as major
# IBKR-accessible equities; significant European-equity
# exposure for Indian investors.
#
# Protocol Section 6 carves out Spanish tax-exempt corporations
# from Article 11(2) for Spain-resident dividend payments
# (Spanish-investor-receiving-Spanish-source case; NOT relevant
# for Indian-resident-receiving-Spanish-source case which is
# the Form 67 scenario).
#
# HU (Article 10(2) 10%; Article 23(2) relief; MFN-list country
# #5 — MFN covers dividends and has NO date cutoff like
# Sweden): verbatim verified against the taxsutra-hosted
# India-Hungary DTAA PDF
# https://www.taxsutra.com/sites/taxsutra.com/files/dtaa/HUNGARY%20DTAA.pdf
# (notified G.S.R. 197(E) dated 2005-03-31; the PDF's line 14
# reads verbatim "Notification : No. GSR 197(E), dated
# 31-3-2005"). The PDF was fetched on 2026-06-06 (111KB; PDF
# 1.4; 14 pages) and pdftotext -layout extracted cleanly.
# Article 10 verbatim text at lines 220-243 (Article 11 begins
# at line 244); Article 23 at lines 446-464 (Article 24 begins
# at line 465); MFN clause in Protocol "With reference to
# Articles 10, 11 and 12" at lines 583-590.
#
# Standard treaty structure: Article 10 = Dividends with a flat
# 10% Article 10(2) cap (no substantial-holder split); Article
# 23 = Elimination of Double Taxation with paragraph 1 =
# Hungary-resident relief with sub-paragraphs (a)(b); paragraph
# 2 = Indian-resident relief (single block — no sub-paragraph
# split unlike India-Israel 24(2)(a) or India-US 25(2)(a));
# paragraph 3 = exempt-income rate-progression applies
# symmetrically. The Schedule FSI relief citation is "Article 23(2)" —
# structurally similar to India-Germany Article 23(2) and
# India-Ireland Article 23(2) which also use a single-paragraph
# Indian-resident credit clause.
#
# Hungary IS the FIFTH MFN-list country vendored (after NL
# #241 / FR #244 / SE #245 / ES #246). Per CBDT Circular
# 3/2022 India has MFN clauses with 6 treaty partners:
# Netherlands / France / Switzerland / Sweden / Spain / Hungary.
# Only Switzerland remains pending of the MFN-list set.
#
# The Protocol "With reference to Articles 10, 11 and 12"
# carries the MFN clause verbatim:
#
#   "In respect of Articles 10 (Dividends), 11 (Interest) and
#   12 (Royalties and fees for technical services), if under
#   any Convention, Agreement or Protocol between India and a
#   third State which is a member of the OECD, India limits
#   its taxation at source on dividends, interest, royalties
#   or fees for technical services to a rate lower or a scope
#   more restricted than the rate or scope provided for in
#   this Convention on the said items of income, the same rate
#   or scope as provided for in that Convention. Agreement or
#   Protocol on the said items of income shall also apply
#   under this Convention".
#
# Unlike Spain (whose MFN clause excludes dividends and covers
# only royalties/FTS), Hungary's MFN clause EXPLICITLY
# enumerates dividends — the dividend-MFN-Nestlé framework
# applies symmetrically.
#
# UNIQUE FEATURE: like India-Sweden the India-Hungary MFN
# clause has NO explicit date cutoff (unlike India-France
# "1-9-1989" and India-Netherlands "after the signature of
# this convention"). It applies to "any Convention, Agreement
# or Protocol between India and a third State which is a
# member of the OECD" without temporal bound.
#
# The same 2023 Indian Supreme Court Nestlé-case ruling
# framework (Civil Appeal 1420/2023) applies:
#   (i) MFN auto-application requires the third State to have
#       been an OECD member at the time of its India treaty
#       signature (not retrospectively after joining OECD later);
#   (ii) Even when (i) is met, MFN application requires
#       explicit CBDT notification (India has not issued such
#       a notification for Hungary).
# Vendoring the literal 10% rate per the Supreme Court
# position.
#
# ALSO NOTABLE: the Protocol "With reference to Article 10"
# contains an India-specific distributed-profits-tax (DDT)
# provision verbatim:
#
#   "When the company paying the dividends is a resident of
#   India the tax on distributed profits shall be deemed to be
#   taxed in the hands of the shareholders and it shall not
#   exceed 10 per cent of the gross amount of dividend".
#
# This DDT pass-through cap applies to Hungarian-resident
# shareholders receiving Indian-source dividends (Indian DDT
# is deemed paid by the Hungarian shareholder; thereby
# eligible for relief under Article 23 paragraph 1 in Hungary).
# It is NOT relevant to Indian-resident receiving
# Hungarian-source dividends (the Form 67 scenario).
#
# Hungary has OTP Bank / Richter Gedeon / MOL Group / Magyar
# Telekom / Wizz Air / 4iG as major IBKR-accessible equities.
#
# VG (no_dtaa): checked on 2026-11-11 against CBDT's DTAA list,
# fetched as for KY / BM above. The British Virgin Islands has no
# comprehensive or limited agreement there, active or superseded.
# It is listed only under Tax Information Exchange Agreement (TIEA):
# signed 2011-02-09 and notified under section 90 by Notification
# No. 54/2011 [F. No. 503/10/2009-FTD-I], S.O. 2301(E), dated
# 2011-10-03,
# https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/w/british-virgin-islands-tax-information-exchange-agreement-tiea-1
# Vendored because BVI-incorporated issuers list on US
# exchanges, so a VG ISIN prefix can reach a US-broker
# statement, and the tri-state lookup must distinguish BVI
# from unvendored countries. Structurally identical to the
# existing no_dtaa entries for Bermuda (BM) and Cayman
# Islands (KY). BVI levies no income tax on
# dividends so the §91 doubly-taxed-income precondition is
# typically not met. In practice BVI-incorporated entities
# trade as US ADRs / NYSE / NASDAQ listings and the dividend
# WHT for such instruments is the US WHT (paid by the
# US-listed entity), not a BVI WHT.
#
# DTAA-coverage scope note (per user directive 2026-06-06):
# vendoring is bounded by countries that users' statements
# actually need, not by theoretical IBKR exposure breadth.
# Remaining DTAA partners (CH / AU / KR / MU / GB / CN / TH /
# ZA / etc.) are tracked in a follow-up GitHub issue rather
# than speculatively vendored here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Country coverage expansion process
#
# To add a new country row, the contributor MUST verify the article
# numbers + rate against the official DTAA text (CBDT's DTAA list
# at https://incometaxindia.gov.in/Pages/international-taxation/dtaa.aspx
# links to the original notification PDFs). The source_citation
# field MUST cite the specific notification or treaty-text URL; do
# NOT cite secondary commentary as the primary source.
#
# Common IBKR-popular countries pending coverage (filed as follow-
# ups; reviewers may request these in-PR if the verification scope
# is bounded):
#
#   * GB / United Kingdom (Article 11 Dividends per India-UK DTAA;
#     rates per the 2013 protocol)
#   * SG / Singapore (Article 10 Dividends per India-Singapore
#     DTAA; 2016 protocol amended capital-gains regime, dividend
#     rates carry forward from the original treaty)
#   * CA / Canada (Article 10 Dividends per India-Canada DTAA)
#   * AU / Australia (Article 10 Dividends per India-Australia DTAA)
#   * CH / Switzerland (Article 10 Dividends per India-Switzerland
#     DTAA; 2010 protocol amended other articles, dividend rate
#     unchanged)
#   * DE / Germany, JP / Japan, NL / Netherlands,
#     IE / Ireland
#
# Adding any of these requires checking the original treaty PDF for
# subclause numbering (it varies between treaties — UK uses
# Article 11 for Dividends, not Article 10) and any dual-rate
# carve-outs.
